============================================================
  X-18 RUN-TO-RUN SCATTER -- three ADDITIONAL runs of the SAME committed
  binary on the reference device, taken because the entry originally quoted
  only WITHIN-RUN spread.
============================================================

  Why this file exists
  --------------------
  X-18's first table reported spreads of 0.04-3.4% and concluded that "every gap
  is far outside its own noise". That spread is measure_util.hpp's WITHIN-run
  scatter -- (max - min) / median over five interleaved batches of one process.
  Run-to-run scatter is a separate and sometimes larger number, and X-17 recorded
  that re-runs move ratios; X-14 took three device runs for the same reason. The
  crossover claim ("they cross over between 7 and 15") rested on the blockSize 7
  row, whose net gap is 3.1% -- small enough that one run could not settle it.

  These are runs 2, 3 and 4. Run 1 is corner_benchmark_pi4.log. Same binary, same
  device, same protocol, throttled=0x0 before AND after each.

  Summary across all FOUR runs (ns/pixel, and the three ratios)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
   blk variant              min       med       max   run-to-run
     3 sliding          101.253   101.518   101.543       0.29%
     3 recompute         84.833    84.840    84.884       0.06%
     3 recompute-col     94.465    94.771    95.185       0.76%
       net (rec/slide)          0.8355 .. 0.8378  median 0.836  scatter 0.28%
       incremental (col/slide)  0.9307 .. 0.9401  median 0.933  scatter 1.00%
       traversal (col/rec)      1.1129 .. 1.1220  median 1.117  scatter 0.82%

     7 sliding          142.538   142.895   143.061       0.37%
     7 recompute        138.553   138.601   138.636       0.06%
     7 recompute-col    147.984   148.528   148.582       0.40%
       net (rec/slide)          0.9691 .. 0.9723  median 0.970  scatter 0.34%
       incremental (col/slide)  1.0382 .. 1.0395  median 1.039  scatter 0.13%
       traversal (col/rec)      1.0677 .. 1.0720  median 1.072  scatter 0.39%

    15 sliding          252.892   253.370   253.715       0.32%
    15 recompute        278.781   278.901   279.453       0.24%
    15 recompute-col    287.873   288.216   288.962       0.38%
       net (rec/slide)          1.1004 .. 1.1024  median 1.101  scatter 0.18%
       incremental (col/slide)  1.1366 .. 1.1397  median 1.138  scatter 0.27%
       traversal (col/rec)      1.0322 .. 1.0340  median 1.034  scatter 0.18%

    31 sliding          558.885   566.677   581.440       3.98%
    31 recompute        687.344   693.688   704.390       2.46%
    31 recompute-col    697.786   702.791   711.910       2.01%
       net (rec/slide)          1.2026 .. 1.2432  median 1.222  scatter 3.32%
       incremental (col/slide)  1.2208 .. 1.2595  median 1.237  scatter 3.13%
       traversal (col/rec)      1.0107 .. 1.0152  median 1.013  scatter 0.45%

  What the extra runs changed, and what they did not
  --------------------------------------------------
  * RUN-TO-RUN IS LARGER THAN WITHIN-RUN AT blockSize 31 and comparable below it.
    Within-run spread at 31 was 1.9-3.8%; run-to-run is 2.0-4.0% on the absolute
    numbers and 3.3% on the net ratio. The original entry's "far outside its own
    noise" was therefore quoting the wrong noise. At 3, 7 and 15 the run-to-run
    scatter is 0.06-0.76%, smaller than the within-run figure.
  * THE RANKING IS PRESERVED IN EVERY RUN, AT EVERY BLOCK SIZE. Net ratio never
    reaches 1.00 at 3 or 7 (max 0.838, 0.972) and never falls to 1.00 at 15 or 31
    (min 1.100, 1.203). The crossover between 7 and 15 is a measured boundary, not
    a one-run artefact -- which is what these runs were taken to find out.
  * THE blockSize 7 ROW SURVIVES. Its net gap is 3.1%, against a combined
    run-to-run range of 0.52 ns/px (sliding) and 0.08 ns/px (recompute) on a
    4.3 ns/px gap -- roughly seven times the scatter.
  * ONE CLAIM IN THE ORIGINAL ENTRY IS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED BY THESE RUNS.
    "The incremental state itself is a loss below blockSize 15" does not follow
    from the numbers even in run 1: the incremental-alone ratio is 1.04x at
    blockSize 7, a WIN, and it replicates at 1.0382-1.0395 across four runs with
    0.13% scatter. The INCREMENTAL effect crosses over between 3 and 7; the NET
    effect crosses between 7 and 15, because the column-major traversal the
    accumulator forces (1.072x at 7) more than eats the incremental win there.
    That distinction is the whole reason the recompute-col control exists, and the
    conclusion is unchanged: at seal_params.yaml's blockSize 3 the shipped sliding
    sweep is 1.20x slower than a plain row-major recomputation.


